---
// Privacy: what Inferview stores, where, and how to delete it. The Firebase sections appear only when the build has
// Firebase settings; the Firestore location sentence appears only when PUBLIC_FIREBASE_REGION is set.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { firebaseConfigured, firebaseRegion } from '../lib/firebase.ts';
---
<Base title="Privacy" description="What Inferview stores, where it is stored, and how to delete it." active="none">
  <div class="wrap page narrow prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>Inferview has no server of its own, no analytics, no advertising and no tracking cookies. This page lists everything that is stored about you and how to remove it.</p>

    <h2>Stored in your browser</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>inferview-progress</code> in localStorage: your progress document. For each lesson, problem or lab step you touched it holds a status (seen or done), your last self-rating, the next review date and the interval in days that produced it, a count and a timestamp, plus your streak (a count and the date of your last graded solve). For each lab you opened it also holds the step you reached and the code in the lab editor.</li>
      <li><code>inferview-theme</code>: light or dark, if you picked one.</li>
      {firebaseConfigured && <li><code>inferview-signed-in</code>: a flag that tells the site to load Firebase on the next visit, set only after you sign in.</li>}
      {firebaseConfigured && <li>When you are signed in, the Firebase SDK keeps its session in your browser's IndexedDB so you stay signed in.</li>}
    </ul>

    {firebaseConfigured && (
      <>
        <h2>Stored with Google Firebase, only if you sign in</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Firebase Authentication keeps a user record for your Google sign-in: a Firebase user id, your Google account identifier, your email address, display name and profile photo URL as Google shares them, and the times of account creation and last sign-in.</li>
          <li>Cloud Firestore keeps one document, <code>users/&lt;your user id&gt;</code>, with the same progress document described above and nothing else. Security rules allow only you, signed in, to read or write it, and reject any document that does not have exactly that shape.</li>
          {firebaseRegion && <li>Where: the Inferview Firebase project, Firestore location <code>{firebaseRegion}</code>.</li>}
          <li>Firebase Authentication data is processed by Google as described in the <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase privacy and security page</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>Other services your browser talks to</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Vercel hosts the site and, like any web host, processes request logs (IP address, user agent, the page requested).</li>
      <li>When you run Python on a code problem or a lab, your browser downloads Pyodide from cdn.jsdelivr.net. Your code runs in your browser and is not sent anywhere{firebaseConfigured ? ', apart from lab code, which syncs with your progress document when you are signed in' : ''}.</li>
      {firebaseConfigured && <li>When you sign in, your browser talks to Google and Firebase servers to complete the sign-in and sync.</li>}
    </ul>

    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    {firebaseConfigured ? (
      <p>On the <a href="/account">account page</a>, "Delete all my data" removes your progress from this browser and, if you are signed in, deletes your Firestore document and your Firebase Authentication record. Signing out alone keeps both. You can also clear this site's data in your browser settings, which removes the local copy only.</p>
    ) : (
      <p>On the <a href="/account">data page</a>, "Delete all my data" removes your progress from this browser. Clearing this site's data in your browser settings does the same.</p>
    )}
    <p>Export your progress from the same page at any time; the file contains exactly what is stored.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
